Supreme People's Court

Opinion on Strengthening and Regulating the Work of State Judicial Assistance by People's Courts

Fa Fa [2016] No. 16

To strengthen and regulate the work of state judicial assistance for people in need during trial and enforcement, protect the lawful rights and interests of parties, and promote social harmony and stability, in accordance with the "Opinion on Establishing and Improving the State Judicial Assistance System (Trial)" issued by the Political and Legal Affairs Commission of the CPC Central Committee, the Ministry of Finance, the Supreme People's Court, the Supreme People's Procuratorate, the Ministry of Public Security, and the Ministry of Justice, and in light of the actual work of the people's courts, the following opinion is put forward.

Article 1. In the course of trial and enforcement, the people's courts may adopt one-time supplementary relief measures for parties whose rights have been infringed and who are unable to obtain effective compensation, where the circumstances specified in this Opinion are met, in order to resolve their urgent livelihood difficulties.

Article 2. State judicial assistance work shall adhere to the principles of fairness, openness, and timeliness, and strictly adhere to the standards and conditions for relief.

Only one-time state judicial assistance shall be provided to the same applicant for the same case. Where compensation can be obtained through litigation, it shall generally be resolved through litigation channels.

Where an applicant meets the conditions for relief, regardless of whether his registered residence is within the jurisdiction of the people's court accepting the case, the court with jurisdiction over the case shall be responsible for providing relief. For state judicial assistance cases that have a significant impact within the jurisdiction and involve a large amount of relief funds, the higher and lower people's courts may provide coordinated relief.

Article 3. Where a party applies for state judicial assistance due to urgent livelihood difficulties, relief shall be granted in any of the following circumstances:

(1) The victim of a criminal offense suffers serious injury or severe disability due to the criminal infringement, and is unable to obtain compensation through litigation and falls into livelihood difficulties because the perpetrator has died or lacks the ability to compensate;

(2) The victim of a criminal offense is in a life-threatening situation due to the criminal infringement and urgently needs medical treatment but cannot afford the medical expenses;

(3) The victim of a criminal offense dies as a result of the criminal infringement, and the close relatives who mainly relied on the victim's income for their livelihood are unable to obtain compensation through litigation and fall into livelihood difficulties because the perpetrator has died or lacks the ability to compensate;

(4) The victim of a criminal offense suffers significant property losses due to the criminal infringement, and is unable to obtain compensation through litigation and falls into livelihood difficulties because the perpetrator has died or lacks the ability to compensate;

(5) A whistleblower, witness, or appraiser suffers personal injury or significant property losses due to retaliation for whistleblowing, testifying, or appraising, and is unable to obtain compensation through litigation and falls into livelihood difficulties;

(6) In cases involving claims for alimony, support, or child support, the applicant for enforcement falls into livelihood difficulties because the person subject to enforcement lacks the ability to perform;

(7) Personal injury is caused by civil torts such as road traffic accidents, and the victim is unable to obtain compensation through litigation and falls into livelihood difficulties;

(8) Other persons whom the people's court deems, based on actual circumstances, to need relief.

For persons involved in litigation-related petitions whose claims have some reasonableness but are difficult to resolve through legal channels and who are in livelihood difficulties, and who are willing to cease petitioning after receiving state judicial assistance, relief may be granted by reference to this Opinion.

Article 4. Relief shall generally not be granted to an applicant who falls under any of the following circumstances:

(1) The applicant bears significant fault for the occurrence of the case;

(2) Without justifiable reason, the applicant refuses to cooperate in ascertaining the facts of the case;

(3) The applicant intentionally makes false statements or forges evidence, obstructing litigation;

(4) During trial or enforcement, the applicant voluntarily waives a claim for civil compensation or refuses compensation from the tortfeasor or his close relatives;

(5) The livelihood difficulties are not caused by the case;

(6) The applicant has already received reasonable compensation or relief through social assistance measures;

(7) The application for relief is submitted by a legal person or other organization;

(8) Other circumstances where relief should not be granted.

Article 5. State judicial assistance primarily takes the form of payment of relief funds, and shall be combined with ideological guidance, coordinated with legal aid and litigation relief, and linked with other social assistance.

Article 6. The relief fund shall be determined based on the average monthly wage of employees in the province, autonomous region, or municipality directly under the Central Government where the court with jurisdiction over the case is located for the previous year, and shall generally not exceed the total of 36 months' average monthly wages.

Where the loss is particularly serious and the livelihood is particularly difficult, requiring an appropriate increase beyond the relief limit, strict review and control shall be exercised, and the relief amount shall not exceed the amount that the people's court should have awarded according to the judgment or the amount that was awarded but not yet enforced.

Article 7. The specific amount of relief funds shall be determined by comprehensively considering the following factors:

(1) The actual losses suffered by the applicant for relief;

(2) Whether the applicant for relief has any fault and the degree of such fault;

(3) The financial situation of the applicant for relief and his family;

(4) The minimum expenditure necessary for the applicant for relief to maintain a basic standard of living at his place of residence;

(5) The actual compensation paid by the compensation obligor.

Article 8. Where the trial or enforcement department of a people's court considers that a party to the case meets the conditions for relief, it shall inform the party that he has the right to apply for state judicial assistance. If the party files an application, the trial or enforcement department shall promptly transfer the relevant materials to the case-filing department.

If a party directly files an application for state judicial assistance with the case-filing department of a people's court, and upon review it is confirmed that the application meets the conditions for relief, the case shall be filed.

Article 9. An application for state judicial assistance shall be submitted in writing; if the applicant truly has difficulty submitting a written application, an oral application may be made, and the people's court shall make a record.

An applicant for relief shall generally submit the following materials when applying for state judicial assistance:

(1) A written application for relief, which shall state the amount of relief requested and the reasons;

(2) Identification of the applicant for relief;

(3) Evidence of actual losses;

(4) Evidence of the livelihood difficulties of the applicant for relief and his family members;

(5) Relevant evidence of whether other compensation or relief has been obtained;

(6) Other materials that can prove the applicant's need for relief.

If the applicant for relief truly cannot provide complete materials, the reasons shall be explained.

Article 10. Evidence of livelihood difficulties of the applicant for relief mainly refers to certificates issued by the village (neighborhood) committee of the applicant's registered residence or habitual residence, or by his work unit, concerning the family population, labor capacity, employment status, family income, and other circumstances of the applicant for relief.

Article 11. A people's court shall establish a Judicial Assistance Committee composed of departments such as case-filing, criminal trial, civil trial, administrative trial, trial supervision, enforcement, state compensation, and finance, responsible for the court's state judicial assistance work. The Judicial Assistance Committee shall have an office, and the office of the Compensation Committee of the people's court shall exercise its functions.

The office of the Compensation Committee of the people's court, as the daily working department of the Judicial Assistance Committee, is responsible for leading, coordinating, and handling the daily affairs of state judicial assistance, executing the resolutions of the Judicial Assistance Committee, and handling state judicial assistance cases.

In basic-level people's courts, the functional institution responsible for state compensation work shall assume the duties of the office of the Judicial Assistance Committee.

Article 12. The decision on relief shall be made within ten working days from the date of case filing. For complex relief cases, the time limit may be appropriately extended with the approval of the court leadership.

A state judicial assistance decision shall be made in writing, affixed with the seal of the people's court. The state judicial assistance decision shall be served promptly.

If the conditions for relief are not met or circumstances for denial exist, the decision to deny relief shall be promptly communicated to the applicant for relief, and explanations shall be provided.

Article 13. Where relief is decided, the formalities shall be completed within seven working days in accordance with relevant financial regulations. After receiving the relief funds allocated by the financial department, the applicant for relief shall be notified within two working days to collect the relief funds.

For applicants with special circumstances such as urgent medical treatment, relief funds may be advanced based on the relief standards, and the approval procedures shall be completed promptly after the relief is provided.

Article 14. Relief funds shall generally be distributed in a lump sum. Under special circumstances, they may be distributed in installments.

When distributing relief funds, the nature of the relief funds, the reasons for granting relief, and the legal consequences of defrauding relief funds shall be explained to the applicant for relief, and a record shall be made and signed by the applicant. If necessary, staff from the village (neighborhood) committee of the applicant's registered residence or habitual residence, or from his work unit, may be invited to witness the distribution of relief funds.

The people's court may, based on the specific circumstances of the applicant for relief, entrust organizations such as the civil affairs department, the township people's government or sub-district office, the village (neighborhood) committee, or the applicant's work unit to distribute the relief funds.

Article 15. People's courts at all levels shall actively coordinate with the financial department to include state judicial assistance funds in the budget, and shall establish a dynamic adjustment mechanism for state judicial assistance funds jointly with the financial department.

For state judicial assistance funds donated by citizens, legal persons, and other organizations, the people's court shall use them strictly and properly, promptly publicize the specific recipients of the assistance, and inform the donors of the assistance situation, ensuring transparency and fairness in the use of assistance funds.

Article 16. The Judicial Assistance Committee of the people's court shall submit a written report on the judicial assistance work of the court for the previous year within one month after the end of the year, and shall accept supervision from disciplinary inspection, supervision, and audit departments as well as higher people's courts, ensuring that funds are used exclusively for their designated purposes.

Article 17. People's courts shall strengthen the informatization construction of state judicial assistance work, incorporate state judicial assistance cases into the trial management information system, promptly record case information, achieve information sharing among courts at four levels, and actively explore the establishment of information-sharing mechanisms for assistance with social security institutions and other relevant assistance institutions.

Higher courts shall guide and supervise the state judicial assistance work of lower courts, preventing imbalances and duplication of assistance.

Article 18. Where personnel of a people's court engage in any of the following acts, they shall be subject to criticism and education; if it constitutes a disciplinary violation, disciplinary sanctions shall be imposed according to relevant regulations; if it constitutes a crime, criminal liability shall be pursued according to law:

(1) Abusing power to decide to grant relief to an applicant who clearly does not meet the conditions;

(2) Falsely reporting or withholding relief funds from the applicant;

(3) Embezzling or misappropriating relief funds;

(4) Failing to promptly handle relief formalities for an applicant who meets the conditions, causing serious consequences;

(5) Other acts in violation of this Opinion.

Article 19. Where the work unit or grassroots organization of the applicant for relief issues false certificates, enabling an applicant who does not meet the conditions to obtain relief, the people's court shall suggest that the relevant unit or its superior competent authority handle the responsible person according to law and discipline.

Article 20. After the applicant for relief receives relief, if the people's court recovers compensation or other execution funds from the person subject to enforcement, the relief funds already distributed shall be deducted from the execution funds.

If the applicant for relief defrauds relief funds by means such as providing false materials, the people's court shall recover them; if it constitutes a crime, criminal liability shall be pursued according to law.

If an applicant for relief in a litigation-related petition receives relief funds but violates the commitment to cease petitioning, the people's court shall recover the relief funds.

Article 21. For applicants for relief who are not covered by state judicial assistance or who still face livelihood difficulties after receiving state judicial assistance, if they meet the conditions for social assistance, the people's court shall, through the linkage mechanism between state judicial assistance and social assistance, coordinate with relevant departments to include them in the scope of social assistance.

July 1, 2016
